  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSprawling high pressure over our region is the primary driver of \n(no) weather heading into the new week. A highly amplified upper-\nlevel ridge axis extends along the Mississippi River this afternoon, \nwhile the axis of the surface high pressure system over the \nNortheast US extends along the Appalachians and over the Gulf Coast. \nSubsidence and continued dryness has allowed for temps today to warm \ninto upper 70s and lower 80s with a light east wind. Have retained\nthe \"Elevated\" fire danger graphic for the persistent low humidity\nand warm temperatures that have stressed and dried out vegetation\ntransitioning out of the growing season. The ridge will be \nimpressed upon by a shortwave trough to our northwest on Tuesday, \nbut do not expect the ridge to be suppressed until mid to late \nweek as western CONUS troughing deepens. Ridge will still be \nprominent over the southeastern CONUS, and the details of the \npattern to our west will be harder to nail down this far out. The \ngeneral idea in the guidance though is that at least some moist \nreturn flow and possible interaction with a shortwave/cold front \nwould be centered around next weekend. Official forecast is an \noptimistic 15-25% chance for rain in the very final period of this\nforecast - next Saturday daytime. The synoptic pattern to our \nwest though is highly uncertain with the injection of a strong jet\nstreak into positively tilted trough over the West Coast. Even at\n7 days out, guidance begins to diverge over how to resolve the \nfeature as one or as distinct waves. Regardless of any rain \nchances, temperatures for the upcoming Halloween day/night may \npush 10 degrees warmer than normal. /NF/",
